A few things worth knowing before you run it:
The two external tools you need:

UAssetGUI — download the latest release from github.com/atenfyr/UAssetGUI/releases. The app auto-detects it if it's in C:\Tools\UAssetGUI\ or on your PATH.
UnrealPak.exe — ships with the free TSW Editor (available from Dovetail via Steam). The app looks for it next to the TSW install automatically.

The timetable JSON schema varies by route. The parser does best-effort field aliasing (handling ServiceName, service_name, Name etc.), but if a route uses unusual field names you may get empty stops. Run with --verbose to see which assets are found and any parse warnings, then tweak uasset/parse.go's firstKey(...) alias lists to match.
The UE version flag passed to UAssetGUI is VER_UE4_27 — correct for TSW6. If you're also extracting older TSW content, you may need VER_UE4_26 for some routes.
